# Article: Project

## Language and layout

- Language: Ruby 4.0 / Rails 8.1 (omakase: SQLite, Propshaft, import
  maps, Hotwire, Solid Queue/Cache/Cable, Minitest)
- Source layout: standard Rails — production code in `app/` and `lib/`,
  tests in `test/` mirroring the source tree, fixtures in `test/fixtures/`

## Commands

- Unit tests: `bin/rails test` (fast lane — model, controller, unit;
  never system tests)
- Coverage: `COVERAGE=1 bin/rails test` — writes `coverage/lcov.info`
- Acceptance suite: `bin/rails test:system` (Capybara + headless
  browser; slow lane only)
- Lint/format: `bin/rubocop` (omakase + rubocop-minitest)

## Quality bars

Universal — gate every acceptance and handoff:

- All unit tests green: `bin/rails test`
- Lint clean: `bin/rubocop`
- CRAP within bar (fresh coverage first):
  `COVERAGE=1 bin/rails test && crap4rb --lcov coverage/lcov.info app/ lib/`
- No new duplication: `jscpd --min-tokens 50 --reporters console app/ lib/`

Role-specific additions:

- qa: acceptance flows for the slice via `bin/rails test:system`
- hardener: none yet — mutation testing lands with the rapid-log
  parser slice (mutant-minitest; see PLAN.md)

## Code style

- Rails omakase throughout; `bin/rubocop` enforces mechanically
- Minitest as Rails ships it: plain assertions, fixtures over
  factories, real objects against the test database. Doubles only at
  true external edges (network, clock); Mocha if unavoidable. No
  RSpec, no factory_bot
- Entry ids are client-generatable UUIDv7 — never rely on
  auto-increment identity

## Notes

- PLAN.md at the repo root is the living plan: current phase, slice
  statuses, decisions. Read it before starting any task; the task's
  slice defines your scope.
- ARCHITECTURE.md at the repo root is the sync design. The schema
  decisions in it (logs are Entry residency queries, movement is an
  append-only successor chain, soft deletes, hlc/server_seq columns) bind
  even in slices that do not implement sync.
- Visual spec: the mockups in github.com/bandoyer/bujo-mockups (the
  mobile artboards are the phone PWA's look in paper-light and Tokyo
  Night-dark). Match them, including the rapid-logging glyphs: • task,
  x done, > migrated, < scheduled, ○ event, – note, * priority signifier.
  Every visible text role uses the reader's selected hand face; any mono
  stack puts that hand first and keeps its mono face only as glyph fallback.
  A page's title is always its first visible text, with context beneath it.
- There is no public registration, but tenant isolation is binding. Every
  read and mutation begins from `Current.user`'s relation; foreign, missing,
  and soft-deleted records must not disclose or affect another journal.
